        "text": "We have presented the first method using diffusion models for causal discovery. Since DPMs approximate the score of the data distribution, they can be used to compute the log-likelihood\u2019s Hessian by backpropagating each element of the output with respect to each element of the input. Thus, DPMs can be used for discovering leaf nodes by identifying elements in the Hessian\u2019s diagonal with a variance of zero across data samples. Applying the leaf finding algorithm d (number of nodes) times, one can discover the topological ordering of the true causal graph. At each iteration, the number of nodes decreases. We avoid re-training the score model by adjusting the score to remove the contribution of the leaf most recently removed. Our empirical results show that neural networks in this setting can be efficiently used for topological ordering in high-dimensional graphs (up to 500 nodes) and with large datasets (up to 105 samples).\nFuture Work. Denoising diffusion is an efficient way of learning the score of a distribution and computing its Jacobian. However, the method for computing the deciduous score developed in Section 3 can be used with other techniques where differentiating the score to obtain the Hessian matrix is a faster option than learning a new score, e.g. sliced score matching (Song et al., 2020) and approximate backpropagation (Kingma & Cun, 2010). In addition, rephrasing the causal discovery problem as topological ordering is not enough for learning the complete causal graph; spurious edges need to be pruned as a post-processing step. Therefore, future work could more comprehensively explore which feature selection methods are best suited for this task. Moreover, DPMs are generative models. Generative diffusion models in the context of causal estimation have been explored by Sanchez & Tsaftaris (2022). In this work, we have not explored the generative aspect, we have solely used DPMs as an approximation of the score."
