    "abstractText": "Discovering causal relations from observational data becomes possible with additional assumptions such as considering the functional relations to be constrained as nonlinear with additive noise. In this case, the Hessian of the data log-likelihood can be used for finding leaf nodes in a causal graph. This observation results in an iterative algorithm for topological ordering which sequentially finds and removes leaf nodes in a graph. Topological ordering approaches for causal discovery are efficient because they reduce the search from graph space to a permutation space. Inspired by recent innovations in diffusion probabilistic models (DPMs), we propose DiffAN, a topological ordering algorithm that leverages DPMs for computing the Hessian. In particular, we update the learned Hessian without re-training the neural network and perform ordering over a batch, which allows scaling to datasets with more samples and variables. We show empirically that our method scales exceptionally well to datasets with up to 500 nodes and up to 10 samples while still performing on par over small datasets with state-of-the-art causal discovery methods.",
